Use derivative information with explicit interval and error bounds (@math-local-error: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Build-accumulation-from-signed-sums-and-controlled-partitions.lingua#r_09B6DB4F5814115AF4BB7BD575") { r_05FA61B1A67F2964C959D41629
Local differentiability says that a finite increment has the form f(a+h)=f(a)+f′(a)h+h e(h), with e(h)→0. The linear part is useful only relative to a requested accuracy and an admissible neighborhood. A small numerical h with no units, scale or bound is not a complete error argument. The shared [Small changes, derivatives, and first-order approximations](../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/models/Small-changes-and-derivatives.lingua#r_4T51QH8VRCNNMY5WD30VJS5P52) square example has an exact remainder; now connect such local reasoning to an interval-wide theorem.

First establish the mean value theorem. For a function continuous on [a,b] and differentiable on (a,b), there is an interior c with f′(c)=[f(b)−f(a)]/(b−a). Here a<b. The extreme-value theorem from the previous lesson supplies attained extrema on the closed interval. At an interior differentiable maximum, the right difference quotients are nonpositive and the left quotients nonnegative, so their common limit is zero; the minimum case is analogous. Consequently, if endpoint values agree, either the function is constant or a nonconstant maximum/minimum occurs inside and has derivative zero. This is Rolle's theorem. For unequal endpoint values, subtract the line joining the endpoints; Rolle's theorem for the resulting function yields the displayed slope. Every step uses the stated continuity and differentiability conditions.

If |f′(x)|≤M throughout the open interval between two inputs, with continuity at its endpoints, the theorem gives |f(b)−f(a)|≤M|b−a|. If f′=0 throughout a connected interval, applying the theorem to any two of its points shows f is constant there. The connected interval matters: a function separately constant on disjoint domain pieces need not have the same constant on every piece.

An error bound for a tangent needs more than a bound on f′ alone. Suppose f′ exists throughout a neighborhood and |f′(x)−f′(a)|≤η there. Apply the mean value theorem to R(x)=f(x)−f(a)−f′(a)(x−a). It gives |R(a+h)|≤η|h| for increments staying in that neighborhood. This makes the needed control explicit. Stronger bounds can follow from an exact remainder or further smoothness; they must be derived or stated with their hypotheses.

For the study polynomial V(s)=5+s³/3−s, its derivative is s²−1 and its exact remainder at a is V(a+h)−V(a)−(a²−1)h=a h²+h³/3. Thus the absolute remainder is at most h²(|a|+|h|/3). At a=1 the tangent is constant, but the actual change is h²+h³/3. For h=0.1 the error is 31/3000≈0.010333 in the volume's litre coordinate. For all |h|≤0.09 the bound is 8343/1000000<0.01. Near this stationary point, a relative error expressed as a fraction of the predicted change is undefined because that prediction is zero; an absolute error statement remains meaningful.

**Read and defend:** [Calculus and differential-equation preparation — Strang and Herman, OpenStax](../../chemistry/industrial-chemistry/sources/Calculus-and-Differential-Equations-OpenStax.lingua#r_027WKVM5NG5X84M86TSVRA77J3), Volume 1 sections 4.2–4.4. Reconstruct the theorem argument and identify its accepted real-number foundation. Compare the error interval above with a tangent at a different point. The function |x−1| on [0,2] has equal endpoint values but no zero derivative where it is differentiable; identify the failed hypothesis before invoking Rolle's theorem. A statement about a mathematical approximation remains separate from uncertainty in inputs and discrepancy between a model and observations.

